From: eirikr <iamhumanipromise1010101@gmail.com>
Date: 2026-05-13
Subject: [PATCH gnumach] ipc: bump ipc_kernel_map_size from 8 MB to 64 MB

hi everyone, this is a small patch I'd like to propose, please bear
with me as this is my first gnumach patch and I'm pretty sheepish
about it.

so I was setting up debian gnu/hurd inside a podman+qemu container
just to play around with it on my linux machine, and during boot
I kept seeing the console flood with:

	no more room in ffffffffdff58888 (xargs(1838))
	no more room in ffffffffdff58888 (xargs(1838))
	[...]

this happens when init's rc-script run-parts forks a bunch of small
helper processes in a burst (xargs, awk, grep in pipelines).  the
"no more room" message comes from mach_port_names() in
ipc/mach_port.c when vm_allocate() on ipc_kernel_map fails.

reading the code, ipc_kernel_map is created as an 8 MB submap of
kernel_map, and the size is a compile-time constant:

	const vm_size_t ipc_kernel_map_size = 8 * 1024 * 1024;

on x86_64 with the wider kernel VA space, 8 MB feels really tight
for what's effectively the per-task port-name allocation arena.
raising it to 64 MB makes my boot quiet (no more flood) and I can't
notice any rss/cost increase because kmem_submap is lazy.

so umm here's the patch?

---
 ipc/ipc_init.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/ipc/ipc_init.c b/ipc/ipc_init.c
index 5dc7b5b..3a4f8b1 100644
--- a/ipc/ipc_init.c
+++ b/ipc/ipc_init.c
@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@

 static struct vm_map ipc_kernel_map_store;
 vm_map_t ipc_kernel_map = &ipc_kernel_map_store;
-const vm_size_t ipc_kernel_map_size = 8 * 1024 * 1024;
+const vm_size_t ipc_kernel_map_size = 64 * 1024 * 1024;

 /*
  *	Routine:	ipc_bootstrap
--
2.49.0

happy to revise, split, or just drop this entirely if it's wrong.
I wasn't sure if 64 MB is the right number -- maybe it should be
proportional to physical RAM?  or maybe there's a config knob I
should have used instead of changing the constant?  I'm pretty new
to this so guidance welcome.

reproducer:
* debian-hurd-amd64.qcow2 baseline (Jan 2026)
* qemu `-machine pc -cpu max -smp 2 -m 2048`
* `/etc/rc.local` that runs `run-parts /etc/boot.d` with 10+ scripts
  spawning curl/apt/grep/awk in pipelines.  boot hangs around
  runlevel 2 with the "no more room" loop on unmodified gnumach.
* after applying the patch (and rebuilding `gnumach-image-1.8-amd64-up`
  as a debian package, then dpkg -I + update-grub + reboot), boot
  finishes normally and rc.local completes.

---

**disclaimer**: I used a large language model (claude) while
developing this patch -- specifically for reading the gnumach
source and reasoning about the failure mode.  the patch itself is
trivial (one number change) but I'm a hobbyist hurd user not a
kernel dev, so please don't merge this just because I'm submitting
it -- there might be a better way to do this (e.g. make it
proportional to memsz_lim, or add a boot-arg, or recompute on
demand).  happy to iterate.
